QCOM A630 compute emulator

Runs the existing QCOM runtime and Mesa compiler without a GPU on Linux.
The interpreter models a subset of A6xx instructions, not cycle timing.
Instruction encoding and lowering follow Mesa 461196a1c827769168304ff3f5b36360f16618ca:
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/tree/461196a1c827769168304ff3f5b36360f16618ca/src/freedreno

From a tinygrad checkout with these changes applied:
  python -m venv .venv
  . .venv/bin/activate
  python -m pip install torch==2.9.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
  python -m pip install -e '.[testing_unit,mesa]' pytest-subtests
  PYTHONPATH=. python -m pytest test/mockgpu/qcom -q

Run the unchanged upstream div/sqrt/exp/softmax/matmul tests, including gradients:
  PYTHONPATH=. DEV=MOCK+QCOM:IR3 HCQ_RUNTIME_DEV=PYTHON python -m test.mockgpu.qcom.test_math --backend
  PYTHONPATH=. DEV=MOCK+QCOM:IR3 HCQ_RUNTIME_DEV=CPU python -m test.mockgpu.qcom.test_math --backend
The QCOM workflow runs these commands on Linux with Python 3.12. The CPU-only
PyTorch install avoids installing GPU libraries for the independent reference.

The integration tests launch separate processes for HCQ_RUNTIME_DEV=PYTHON and
CPU, set DEV=MOCK+QCOM:IR3, and check normal teardown. The CPU HCQ runtime needs
clang available. test_workloads.py covers deterministic matrix products,
reduction/elementwise chains and JIT capture/replay with changing inputs.
Raw semantic tests do not require PyTorch; broader upstream backend comparisons do.

To run a workload and print actual dispatch dimensions and shader hashes:
  PYTHONPATH=. DEV=MOCK+QCOM:IR3 HCQ_RUNTIME_DEV=CPU python test/mockgpu/qcom/test_workloads.py matmul_17_33_9
  PYTHONPATH=. DEV=MOCK+QCOM:IR3 HCQ_RUNTIME_DEV=CPU python test/mockgpu/qcom/test_workloads.py jit
Set DUMP_IR3=1 to include the compiled instruction listing.

qcomdriver.py owns KGSL resources and submission transactions; state.py and
compute.py interpret PM4 state and dispatches; ir3.py executes instructions.
ioctl.py bridges compiled HCQ calls into tracked mock file descriptors.
Unsupported instructions, state and resources raise contextual errors. Failed
submissions preserve prior memory/timestamps and permit subsequent valid calls.
Submission execution is single-threaded; this is not a concurrent memory model.

Supported paths include full32 integer and float arithmetic, unfused mad.f32,
rcp/rsq/sqrt/exp2 with ss dependencies, half-source AND, sel.b32, u32/u8 global
memory, shared u32 memory with barriers, and uniform br/jump loops. Arithmetic
and comparisons handle Inf/NaN; numeric NaN results use a canonical quiet NaN.
Subnormal numeric inputs/results and non-finite float-to-integer conversions
remain unsupported. Bit moves and sel.b32 preserve all payload bits.
Full/half register banks remain separate. Divergent branches, arbitrary
predication, atomics and general narrow conversions remain unsupported.
Programs are limited to 4096 encoded instructions and 65536 executed steps per
lane. These are emulator resource policies, not hardware limits.

SFU results use the NIR mathematical operations rounded to binary32. This source
model does not reproduce undocumented hardware approximation errors or NaN payload
selection. Semantic mappings are in ir3_compiler_nir.c:784-799, definitions in
src/compiler/nir/nir_opcodes.py:229-232, and ss hazards in ir3_legalize.c at the
fixed Mesa revision above. ir3.c:1582-1595 supplies the exact FLUT bit patterns.

Compiled fixtures are compiler output, not GPU captures. Offline results do not
require byte-identical output from a newer compiler: production tests check results
and dispatch state, while raw tests replay fixed binaries. fixtures/compute stores
four unique binaries; manifest.json retains the original ten compilation profiles
and scalar execution inputs, expected bytes, dimensions and source identities.
Offline results do not establish hardware agreement. Fixes should include independent instruction tests
and relevant production-path regressions; hardware comparisons are useful
additional evidence when a device is available.
